    "fresh_checks": [
      "Product positivity is defined for a smooth real nonclosed background; no closedness is needed for local quasi-psh envelopes.",
      "Affine time additions have zero Hessian; positive affine reparametrization is holomorphic on strips; maxima, convex combinations and smooth gauge changes preserve the current inequality.",
      "Interior compact intervals admit larger finite interior times; invariant-strip convexity bounds every slice above by their chord.",
      "The minimum principle supplies an integrable lower potential on the larger finite strip; this is a retained Chapter1 input, whose full current-cycle proof was read at checkpoint298.",
      "Almost-everywhere finite vertical convexity gives pointwise time continuity; the integrable lower potential and common upper bound give dominated L1 continuity.",
      "Endpoint L1 convergence admits almost-everywhere convergent time sequences. Convexity and the quasi-psh almost-everywhere order lemma extend the chord bound to every spatial point.",
      "Joint boundary bounds use upper semicontinuity and a vanishing coefficient times an upper-bounded other endpoint; at poles an arbitrary real local upper bound is sent to minus infinity.",
      "The endpoint chord is jointly upper semicontinuous and locally bounded above in the interior, so the regularized supremum remains below it.",
      "An admissible competitor ensures each envelope slice is not identically minus infinity. The local product envelope and imaginary-translation invariance yield a subgeodesic in its own admissible family.",
      "The lower endpoint-attaining competitor and chord give the displayed absolute-value L1 estimate almost everywhere; poles form null sets.",
      "On each finite subinterval the original path attains its own endpoints and is admissible. The larger local Perron envelope glues by the retained boundary gluing lemma and preserves the original endpoint bounds; global maximality forces equality.",
      "For ordinarily equivalent endpoints, the maximum of two affine barriers is a subgeodesic, equals both endpoints and has quantitative L1 endpoint control.",
      "The affine barrier lower bounds and chord upper bounds give the same ordinary singularity type for every slice.",
      "Convex secant slope monotonicity together with both endpoint estimates gives C-Lipschitz time control off the common pole set, including the endpoints.",
      "A common lower potential for different types supplies an admissible constant path but does not establish endpoint attainment without a separate barrier.",
      "The smooth product coefficient matrix has cross factors one half and temporal factor one quarter because time is the real part of the strip coordinate.",
      "Positive definite H gives the Schur-complement positivity and determinant-zero equivalences. The d dc normalization gives the stated gradient-square wedge identity.",
      "Differentiating the action gives the velocity term and n/2 spatial Hessian variation; time integration includes the full derivative n dd^c f wedge T of the evolving volume.",
      "Spatial integration by parts uses the whole form f^2 T. Its product rule includes both first-derivative torsion terms with the displayed signs and f^2 dd^c T; cancellation removes only the f dd^c f terms.",
      "Uniform positive definiteness on the compact space-time cylinder permits all sufficiently small endpoint-fixed smooth variations. The fundamental lemma gives both stationarity implications.",
      "Closed Kahler backgrounds remove all torsion terms; the Gauduchon condition removes the last term only. For nonzero constant velocity, stationarity is equivalent to dd^c eta^(n-1)=0, including the vacuous dimension-one case.",
      "Constant translations equal their endpoint chord and are therefore Perron segments solving the smooth product equation. The retained Chapter4 torus and surface persistence results explain failure of action stationarity without asserting that all surface torsion terms vanish."
    ],
